  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nThe forecast period begins with an upper ridge over Montana and \nWyoming and northwest flow over the Great Plains. Fog has developed \nto our east, across portions of south central Iowa and northern \nMissouri. The big question will be how this impacts the Omaha CWA. \nStratus has held on longer than expected this evening toward Norfolk \nand south to Columbus and Lincoln. As long as the stratus sticks \naround, this will help hinder fog development. We have had some \nclearing south toward Beatrice, Hebron and York. With light \nsoutheasterly winds and clear skies, we are looking at the potential \nfor fog development later this evening/tonight. Some patchy dense \nfog may be possible. To the east of our area, we are seeing fog \ndevelopment toward south central Iowa and northern Missouri. Winds \nare expected to shift, becoming more southerly as we head further \ninto the overnight hours which could also limit potential westward \nspread of fog into the eastern portion of our CWA. \n\nHeading into the weekend, the ridge will shift east, over the Black \nHills by Saturday morning and across the Dakotas and Nebraska by \nSaturday evening. Expect Saturday highs to reach into the mid-90s to \naround 104. Heat index values will be at or above 105 degrees for \nmost areas. Sunday temperatures will be in the mid-90s to low 100s \nfor highs. Sunday and Monday morning lows will not recover well and \nwill be in the mid to upper 70s each morning with heat index values \nin the mid-70s. \n\nA Heat Advisory remains in place for Monona County in Iowa and \nseveral counties across eastern Nebraska from noon Saturday through \n10pm Monday. Expect heat index values to reach as high as 109 during \nthe afternoon hours and the mid to upper 70s overnight. Knox, Cedar, \nAntelope and Pierce counties in northeastern Nebraska can expect \nafternoon heat index values to reach or exceed 110 degrees with \nlittle recovery overnight. Therefore, they have been upgraded to an \nExtreme Heat Warning which will be in effect from noon Saturday to \n7am Monday. The Extreme Heat Watch for Douglas and Sarpy counties in \nNebraska and most of our western Iowa counties will likely need to \nbe upgraded to a warning for Sunday and possibly Monday, with a \nwestward expansion not out of the question.\n\nA shortwave trough is expected drop into eastern Nebraska/western \nIowa Sunday evening, bringing a chance of a few areas getting \nclipped with an isolated shower or thunderstorm. Should any storms \nbe able to develop, they could become strong to marginally severe \nwith large hail and damaging winds being the main concerns. Should \nareas receive any storms, this could help cool things down a little \nbit overnight, allowing temperatures to recover slightly. This would \nlikely have an impact on current forecasted temperatures for Monday. \n\nBeginning Tuesday, we start to see a shift in the pattern with a few \ndisturbances trying to move across the Great Plains. This will give \nus a few isolated chances for showers or storms. The more notable \nchange will be for some slightly cooler, although still hot \ntemperatures. Afternoon high temperatures will range from the low to \nmid-90s each day while lows will range from the upper 60s to low\n70s.",
